1
00:00:00,290 --> 00:00:04,500
Every tenth of November, the oldest Marine
in the room hands a piece of cake to

2
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:05,820
the youngest.

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:11,174
It started here, in 1775:
two battalions of Marines.

4
00:00:11,374 --> 00:00:14,831
Every generation since
has added to the line.

5
00:00:14,881 --> 00:00:21,299
Tripoli. Belleau Wood. Iwo
Jima. Chosin Reservoir.

6
00:00:21,349 --> 00:00:24,183
Huế City. Fallujah.

7
00:00:24,383 --> 00:00:29,253
In 1921, General John A.
Lejeune, 13th Commandant,

8
00:00:29,303 --> 00:00:33,543
ordered a message read on
every birthday. It still is:

9
00:00:33,593 --> 00:00:37,380
'This high name we have received
from those who preceded us.'

10
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:42,270
That's what the cake is.
Nobody earns the title alone.

11
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,580
Keep faith with the Marines
who came before you,

12
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:46,208
and hand it on.

13
00:00:46,418 --> 00:00:50,461
Semper Fidelis. Happy birthday, Marines.
